  {
   "id": "Os89y2y1tsUJ",
   "from": "Matt Hoffman",
   "author": "1004dd6f66bb10c3",
   "date": "2012-10-16T01:56:28Z",
   "subject": "\"discontinuous posterior\" warning cause",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "Hi all,\r<br>\r<br>People have been getting the &quot;is your posterior discontinuous?&quot; error\r<br>message a lot more than I would expect given random initializations.\r<br>The reason is that find_reasonable_parameters() was being called\r<br>before the parameters were initialized, so it was starting with all\r<br>parameters=0.\r<br>\r<br>This bug (which I just pushed a fix for) sometimes results in bad\r<br>behavior. I discovered it while playing with the Cauchy distribution\r<br>like so:\r<br>\r<br>{{{\r<br>parameters {\r<br>  real x;\r<br>  real y;\r<br>}\r<br>transformed parameters {\r<br>  real xovery;\r<br>  xovery &lt;- x / y;\r<br>}\r<br>model {\r<br>  real z;\r<br>\r<br>  x ~ normal(0, 1);\r<br>  y ~ normal(0, 1);\r<br>\r<br>  z &lt;- 0;\r<br>  z ~ normal(xovery, 1);\r<br>}\r<br>}}}\r<br>\r<br>Before, the model crashed, because it was trying to find a reasonable\r<br>epsilon at the setting x=0,y=0 (which yields a nan). Now it works\r<br>fine, because that setting has 0 posterior mass.\r<br>\r<br>BTW, the posterior for x and y in this model (plot attached) looks\r<br>like the top of an opened pre-pull-tab beer can.\r<br>\r<br>Matt\r<br>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "7nHEiGlYdBAJ",
