  {
   "id": "avh4v4Xl-pgJ",
   "from": "Bob Carpenter",
   "author": "8aa9b8cc06fdc99c",
   "date": "2012-10-16T21:39:39Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-dev] asserts [was Re: \"discontinuous posterior\" warning cause]",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "In general, assert() should be used to catch programmer error,\r<br>not to trap user errors.  It&#39;s then typical to turn them off\r<br>&quot;in production&quot;.\r<br>\r<br>1.  Calls to assert() in tests should be replaced with EXPECT_TRUE calls.\r<br>\r<br>2. Calls to assert() elsewhere should be replaced with\r<br>\r<br>   a)  runtime checks that throw exceptions if they are the\r<br>       result of user behavior, or\r<br>\r<br>   b)  nothing if they are programmer error.\r<br>\r<br>An alternative would be to leave the asserts for case 2b in\r<br>place and run with -DNDEBUG option.\r<br>\r<br>I&#39;ll clean these up before the next release.  Any thoughts\r<br>on whether to just systematically remove all uses of assert()\r<br>or not?\r<br>\r<br>- Bob\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>\r<br>On 10/16/12 5:02 PM, Ben Goodrich wrote:\r<br>&gt; On Monday, October 15, 2012 9:56:51 PM UTC-4, Matt Hoffman wrote:\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     This bug (which I just pushed a fix for) sometimes results in bad\r<br>&gt;     behavior.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; This patch adds a couple of asserts(), and we already had a few others:\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; |\r<br>&gt; goodrich@CYBERPOWERPC:/opt/stan$ git grep -n &quot;assert(&quot; src/ | cat\r<br>&gt; src/stan/mcmc/adaptive_cdhmc.hpp:153:        assert(x.size() == _x.size());\r<br>&gt; src/stan/mcmc/adaptive_cdhmc.hpp:154:        assert(z.size() == _z.size());\r<br>&gt; src/stan/mcmc/hmc_base.hpp:142:          assert(params_r-&gt;size() == model.num_params_r());\r<br>&gt; src/stan/mcmc/hmc_base.hpp:146:          assert(params_i-&gt;size() == model.num_params_i());\r<br>&gt; src/stan/mcmc/hmc_base.hpp:168:        assert(x.size() == this-&gt;_x.size());\r<br>&gt; src/stan/mcmc/hmc_base.hpp:169:        assert(z.size() == this-&gt;_z.size());\r<br>&gt; src/stan/meta/traits.hpp:150:    // assert((length(x1) == 1) || (length(x1) == result));\r<br>&gt; src/stan/meta/traits.hpp:151:    // assert((length(x2) == 1) || (length(x2) == result));\r<br>&gt; src/stan/meta/traits.hpp:160:    // assert((length(x1) == 1) || (length(x1) == result));\r<br>&gt; src/stan/meta/traits.hpp:161:    // assert((length(x2) == 1) || (length(x2) == result));\r<br>&gt; src/stan/meta/traits.hpp:162:    // assert((length(x3) == 1) || (length(x3) == result));\r<br>&gt; src/test/io/reader_test.cpp:680:  assert(solver.eigenvalues()[0] &gt; 1E-10);\r<br>&gt; src/test/io/reader_test.cpp:706:  assert(solver.eigenvalues()[0] &gt; 1E-10);\r<br>&gt; src/test/io/reader_test.cpp:769:  assert(solver.eigenvalues()[0] &gt; 1E-10); // check positive definite with smallest\r<br>&gt; eigenvalue &gt; 0\r<br>&gt; src/test/io/reader_test.cpp:795:  assert(solver.eigenvalues()[0] &gt; 1E-10); // check positive definite with smallest\r<br>&gt; eigenvalue &gt; 0\r<br>&gt; |\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; I am not sure what to throw in this case (Daniel?), but if an assert() is triggered it would take down the process if it\r<br>&gt; were called from R. I think asserts get disabled if NDEBUG is defined, but we should probably replace these with\r<br>&gt; something else to be on the safe side.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Ben\r<br>&gt;\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "&gt; --\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>"
